4. Data Types in Go
Basic type: Numbers, strings, and booleans come under this category.
Aggregate type: Array and structs come under this category.
Reference type: Pointers, slices, maps, functions, and channels come under this category.
Interface type

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. There is difference btw Println and Printf:
if we wrote something like this:

a=10;
fmt.Println("%d", a);  //it will print (%d 10)
fmt.Printf("%d",a);  //it will print (10)


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. if we use (%f) for float it will print upto 6 decimal places.
but if we use (%g) it will write upto needed or last non-zero character.